var SaltCore=SaltCore||{};SaltCore["journey-deity-nephthys"]=(()=>{var o=Object.defineProperty;var r=Object.getOwnPropertyDescriptor;var h=Object.getOwnPropertyNames;var d=Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty;var l=(t,e)=>{for(var n in e)o(t,n,{get:e[n],enumerable:!0})},y=(t,e,n,i)=>{if(e&&typeof e=="object"||typeof e=="function")for(let a of h(e))!d.call(t,a)&&a!==n&&o(t,a,{get:()=>e[a],enumerable:!(i=r(e,a))||i.enumerable});return t};var u=t=>y(o({},"__esModule",{value:!0}),t);var f={};l(f,{journey:()=>s});var s={id:"deity-nephthys",kind:"deity",paths:["egyptian"],deityRef:"traditional/deity/nephthys",title:"Nephthys: Lady of the House",summary:"A month with Nebet-Het, the sister who mourned with Isis, the kite whose cry is the sound of grief, and the guardian of the dead at the head of the coffin. Study her name, the Lamentations sung in her voice and how little of her own cult survives, keep ten evening vigils with a lamp, and decide whether to dedicate.",welcome:`This journey spends about a lunar month with Nephthys, the Egyptian goddess who mourns and protects the dead. She is the sister of Isis, Osiris and Set, and in myth she stood beside Isis through the search for Osiris, the mourning and the restoration. She suits people who are grieving, people who sit with the dying or care for the bereaved, people who keep a household together through hard times, and anyone who has stayed faithful in a place where staying was not easy.

You will learn what her name means and how she was shown, her part in the Osiris myth and in Egyptian funerals, the Lamentations of Isis and Nephthys that priestesses sang in her voice, and how she is honoured today. You will also learn how little survives of a cult of her own, and why much of what is written about her as a "dark" or "hidden" goddess is modern. For ten evenings you will keep a short vigil with a lamp. At the end you may dedicate yourself to her, or not.

This is a self-dedication. It is not an initiation into Kemetic Orthodoxy or any other group, and it does not replace one. If you are grieving hard, this journey can sit beside you, but please lean on living people too, and on grief support or a counsellor if you need it.`,about:`Nephthys (Egyptian Nebet-Het, "Lady of the House" or "Lady of the Enclosure") was a daughter of the earth god Geb and the sky goddess Nut, sister of Osiris, Isis and Set, and wife of Set. She is shown as a woman wearing on her head the hieroglyphs of her name: a basket (neb, "lady") set on top of the outline of a great house or enclosure (het). In myth she is above all the companion of Isis: the two sisters searched for the murdered Osiris, mourned over his body as two kites, and helped bring him back to life, and she was a nurse and protector of the child Horus. In funerals two women played Isis and Nephthys and keened over the dead; on coffins she kneels at the head with wings spread, while Isis kneels at the foot; and she guarded the canopic jar of the lungs with the son of Horus called Hapy. In Plutarch's later telling she is the mother of Anubis by Osiris. Few temples of her own are known, and she was usually honoured beside Isis and Osiris rather than alone. Today she is honoured in Kemetic religion, by reconstructionists and in Kemetic Orthodoxy, and by many modern Pagans, often as a goddess of grief, endings and quiet strength.`,notes:["Very little survives of a cult of Nephthys on her own. Most of what we know comes from funerary texts, the Osiris myth and her pairing with Isis. Be honest about that gap rather than filling it with confident invention.",'Much modern writing calls Nephthys a "dark goddess", a "hidden" goddess or the "shadow" of Isis. These are modern interpretations, some of them lovely, but not ancient teaching. Her Egyptian roles were mourner, protector, nurse and guardian.',"The story that Nephthys bore Anubis to Osiris comes from Plutarch (around 100 CE). Earlier Egyptian sources give Anubis other parents, and Nephthys and Set have no children together in most texts.","This journey is devotional and protective. It does not include contacting the dead; Egyptian practice toward the dead was offering, remembrance and care.","Grief: honouring Nephthys can comfort, and it can also stir things up. Keep rites short when you are raw, and reach out to people and professionals when you need them.","Safety: never leave an oil lamp or candle burning unattended or overnight. Keep incense smoke away from pet birds. Do not buy antiquities or animal mummies; reproductions and photographs are better offerings."],holyDays:[],stages:[{kind:"threshold",title:"At the Head of the Bier",intro:"Begin by learning who Nephthys is and making her a quiet shrine with a lamp. The threshold ends with a short introduction rite: you greet her, give water, bread and incense, and, if you wish, name someone you have lost.",days:3,lessons:[{id:"nephthys-who",title:"Nebet-Het, Lady of the House",summary:"Her name and crown, her family, the kite, and her place at the head of the coffin.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"A name that is a house",body:`Her Egyptian name was Nebet-Het, which means "Lady of the House" or "Lady of the Enclosure". "Nephthys" is the Greek form. She wears her name on her head, as Isis wears the throne: a basket, the sign for "lady", set on top of the outline of a large building, the sign for a house, mansion or walled enclosure.

What house? Egyptologists are not sure. It may be a temple enclosure, the precinct of the gods; it may be the house of the dead, the tomb; it may be the household in a wider sense. Modern devotees often hear "lady of the house" as the one who keeps the home together, and many honour her as a goddess of the household. That reading is reasonable and comforting, but it is an interpretation. The name itself is all we have.`},{heading:"The sister",body:`In the theology of Heliopolis, Nephthys was the youngest of the four children of Geb and Nut, born on the last of the five days upon the year. She married her brother Set, and her other brother Osiris married her sister Isis. When Set killed Osiris, Nephthys took her sister's side, not her husband's. That choice is the centre of her story.

From the Pyramid Texts onward, Isis and Nephthys are almost always together. They search for Osiris, find him, mourn him, protect his body and help bring him back to life. Texts often speak of them as "the two sisters" or "the two kites", and on coffins and shrines they mirror each other, one at each end, wings spread.`},{heading:"The kite",body:`The bird of Nephthys is the kite, a bird of prey that still circles over Egyptian towns and fields. Its thin, high, whistling cry sounded to Egyptians like the keening of women at a funeral. In the Pyramid Texts and later, Isis and Nephthys come to Osiris as two kites, crying over him.

At real funerals two women, often members of the family or professional mourners, took the parts of Isis and Nephthys. They were called the "two kites" (djeryt), and they wailed and sang over the coffin as it was carried to the tomb. Tomb paintings show them kneeling at the head and foot of the bier with their hands raised or thrown over their heads.`},{heading:"At the head of the coffin",body:`On coffins and sarcophagi of many periods, Nephthys kneels at the head end, with her wings spread over the dead, while Isis kneels at the foot. On the corners of royal sarcophagi and canopic shrines, like those of Tutankhamun, four goddesses stand guard with outspread arms: Isis, Nephthys, Neith and Serqet. Each also watched over one of the four sons of Horus and the organs in their jars; Nephthys guarded Hapy, who held the lungs.

So her most common place, in surviving Egyptian art, is right beside the dead: at the head, close, watching. That is perhaps the simplest way to know her: the one who stays.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Her name and crown, her family in the Heliopolitan Ennead, her pairing with Isis as mourner and protector, the two kites in the Pyramid Texts and funerary art, and her place on coffins and canopic shrines are well attested from the Old Kingdom onward.",unknown:'What "house" her name refers to is uncertain. Her origins before the Pyramid Texts are unknown, and some scholars have suggested she was created mainly as a counterpart to Isis. How she was understood outside funerary religion is hard to recover.'},inPractice:"Choose a name (Nephthys or Nebet-Het) and an image: a woman crowned with the house-and-basket sign, or kneeling with wings spread, as on a coffin. Choose an oil lamp or candle that will be hers. If you have lost someone, decide whether their photograph will sit near her shrine.",reflect:["Nephthys chose her sister over her husband. Have you ever had to choose between loyalties?","Who has stayed with you at a hard time, at the head of the bier? Whom have you stayed with?"],further:["Richard H. Wilkinson, The Complete Gods and Goddesses of Ancient Egypt (2003), entry on Nephthys","Geraldine Pinch, Egyptian Mythology: A Guide to the Gods, Goddesses, and Traditions of Ancient Egypt (Oxford, 2004)"],library:["traditional/deity/nephthys","traditional/deity/isis","traditional/deity/osiris","traditional/deity/set"]}],practices:[{id:"nephthys-shrine",title:"Set up her shrine and lamp",instructions:`Choose a quiet shelf or small table where you can sit in the evening. Wipe it with water that has a pinch of natron, washing soda or salt dissolved in it.

Lay a clean white or dark blue cloth; linen if you can, the cloth of temples and of the wrapped dead. Set her image at the back. In front, place a bowl for water, a dish for bread, an incense burner on a heatproof tile, and a lamp: a small oil lamp on a heatproof dish, a candle in a glass holder, or a battery lamp. Add, if you like, a strip of white linen, a piece of obsidian or moonstone, a sistrum or small rattle, and photographs of your dead to one side.

If you also honour Isis, you may set the two sisters side by side, as they so often appear. Cover her image with a cloth between visits if you can, as Egyptian shrines were closed when not in use.`,times:1,prompt:"What did you place on her shrine, and whose names or faces, if any?"}],rite:{title:"Introduction to Nephthys",summary:"A short evening visit: purify, light her lamp, greet her by her names, offer water, bread and incense, and tell her why you have come.",minutes:20,ingredients:[{name:"Nephthys",ref:"traditional/deity/nephthys"},{name:"An offering bowl of fresh, cool water",ref:"traditional/vessel/offering_bowl"},{name:"A pinch of natron, washing soda or salt in a cup of water, for washing"},{name:"A piece of bread",ref:"traditional/vessel/dish"},{name:"Myrrh or frankincense incense",ref:"traditional/herb/myrrh"},{name:"An incense burner on a heatproof tile",ref:"traditional/tool/incense_burner"},{name:"An oil lamp, or a white or silver candle",ref:"traditional/candle/silver"},{name:"The name of someone you have lost, if you wish"}],preparation:"Wash your hands and face and rinse your mouth with the natron or salt water, spitting it out. Wear clean clothes. Come in the evening, after dark if you can. Have the shrine clean and her image covered. Turn off your phone.",steps:[{title:"Light and purify",instructions:"Light her lamp, then the incense. Touch the natron water to your lips and pass your hands through the smoke.",light:!0,spoken:"I am pure. My hands are washed and my mouth is clean. I come quietly to the Lady of the House."},{title:"Open the shrine",instructions:"Uncover her image slowly.",spoken:"Your face is uncovered, Nebet-Het. Awake in peace, sister of Aset."},{title:"Adore",instructions:"Raise both hands, palms toward her, at face height, and speak.",spoken:"Homage to you, Nebet-Het, Lady of the House, daughter of Geb and Nut, sister who stayed. You who wept as a kite, who sat at the head of the bier, who spread your wings over the dead: I greet you."},{title:"Offer",instructions:"Lift the water, then the bread, toward her and set them down. Hold the incense up for a moment.",spoken:"An offering for Nebet-Het: cool water, bread and sweet smoke. I give them freely. May they please you."},{title:"Say why you have come",instructions:"Speak in your own words: your name, what drew you to her, and that you will spend a month learning before you decide anything. If you wish, say the name of someone you have lost. Then sit quietly by the lamp.",minutes:5},{title:"Close and withdraw",instructions:"Thank her. Cover her image and put out the lamp. Step back before you turn away.",snuff:!0,spoken:"Rest in peace, Nebet-Het. I will come again."}],closing:"Eat the bread later or the next morning; that is the reversion of offerings. Pour the water on the earth. Write a few lines in your journal, and any name you spoke.",safety:"Keep the lamp, candle and incense on heatproof surfaces in a ventilated room, away from curtains, children, pets and pet birds, and never leave them burning unattended or overnight. Do not swallow the natron water. If grief overwhelms you, stop gently and reach out to someone."}},{kind:"study",title:"The Two Sisters",intro:"Three lessons: Nephthys in the myth of Osiris; the Lamentations sung in her voice; and the traces of her own worship, and what Plutarch made of her. Keep a short evening visit to her shrine in between.",days:11,lessons:[{id:"nephthys-myth",title:"The Search and the Mourning",summary:"Nephthys in the Osiris myth, as nurse of Horus, and in Plutarch\u2019s later telling.",minutes:8,sections:[{heading:"In the Pyramid Texts",body:`The oldest religious texts of Egypt, carved in royal burial chambers from about 2350 BCE, already know Nephthys well. Again and again Isis and Nephthys come together to the dead king, who is identified with Osiris. They find him lying on the riverbank where Set struck him, they mourn him, they gather his limbs and protect his body, and they rouse him. In a plain rendering, the texts say things like: Isis comes, Nephthys comes, one from the west and one from the east, one as a screeching kite and one as a kite; they have found Osiris and they weep over him.

These lines were spoken for the king's own resurrection, and later for every Egyptian who could afford a coffin and the right words. Each dead person was mourned by the two sisters in the person of the two kites, and raised as Osiris was.`},{heading:"Against her husband",body:`Nephthys was the wife of Set, and the myth puts her in an impossible place: her husband has killed her brother. She chooses her brother and sister. She does not take revenge on Set; she simply goes where she is needed, to the body and to her grieving sister. The Egyptian texts do not make much of the marriage, and some scholars think the pairing with Set was mainly a matter of symmetry, two couples for the four children of Geb and Nut.

Still, many modern devotees find in her a woman who left a harmful place and stayed loyal to what was right. That reading goes beyond the sources, but it rests on a real feature of the myth: in every Egyptian text she is on Osiris's side.`},{heading:"Nurse of Horus",body:`After Horus was born, Nephthys helped protect and nurse him. Some texts call her the nurse of the king, as Horus on earth, and in temple scenes she suckles or embraces the young king. In the funerary texts and in the temple scenes of later periods she stands with Isis on Horus's side, never on her husband's.

So her care runs both ways along the line of the family: she mourns and guards the dead father, and she nurses and protects the living son. She is the aunt who is always there.`},{heading:"Plutarch\u2019s Nephthys",body:`In Plutarch's On Isis and Osiris, written in Greek around 100 CE, Nephthys has an added story. Loving Osiris, she lay with him in the dark, perhaps disguised as Isis, and bore a child, Anubis. Fearing Set, she hid the baby, and Isis searched for him with the help of dogs, found him, and raised him as her guardian. Plutarch also gives Greek, philosophical meanings to the sisters: Isis is the fertile land the Nile floods, and Nephthys is the edge of the land, the borders where the desert begins, and the end of things.

The Anubis story is not found in early Egyptian sources, where Anubis has other parents, though some late Egyptian texts do call him the son of Nephthys. Plutarch's allegory is his own. Use him as a late, learned witness, and say so when you tell his version.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Nephthys\u2019s mourning and protection of Osiris appear throughout the Pyramid Texts, Coffin Texts and Book of the Dead; her role as nurse of Horus and of the king appears in temple and royal texts. Plutarch tells the Anubis story in On Isis and Osiris \xA714 and gives his allegory in \xA738.",unknown:"Why Nephthys was paired with Set, and how much the marriage meant to Egyptians, is unclear. The Anubis story may be Plutarch\u2019s or late Egyptian, and earlier sources do not support it. Exactly how the Pyramid Texts were recited is not known."},inPractice:"Read a selection of Pyramid Text utterances that mention Isis and Nephthys in a modern translation (Faulkner\u2019s or James P. Allen\u2019s). When you tell her story at her shrine, say which parts come from Egyptian texts and which from Plutarch. If you honour Anubis too, you may set him near her, knowing that the link is late.",reflect:["Have you ever had to go where you were needed, rather than where you were expected to stand?","Who in your family, by blood or by choice, has been the one who is always there?"],further:["James P. Allen, The Ancient Egyptian Pyramid Texts (2nd edn, 2015)","Plutarch, On Isis and Osiris, trans. F. C. Babbitt (Loeb, 1936; public domain, at LacusCurtius)","Miriam Lichtheim, Ancient Egyptian Literature, vol. 2 (1976), for the Contendings"],library:["traditional/deity/nephthys","traditional/deity/anubis","traditional/deity/horus","traditional/deity/isis"]},{id:"nephthys-lamentations",title:"The Lamentations of Isis and Nephthys",summary:"The songs sung in the sisters\u2019 voices for Osiris, who sang them, and how they were performed.",minutes:8,sections:[{heading:"Two papyri",body:`Two late papyri preserve songs sung in the voices of Isis and Nephthys for Osiris. The Lamentations of Isis and Nephthys, in a papyrus in Berlin (Papyrus Berlin 3008), dates from the Ptolemaic period. The Songs of Isis and Nephthys, in the Bremner-Rhind papyrus in the British Museum, were copied around 312 BCE and translated by Raymond Faulkner in 1936. Both draw on much older material.

The songs are long calls to the dead god to come home: to his house, to his sisters, to his temple. They name his beauty and his titles, they grieve over his absence, and they ask him to return. In a plain rendering, one refrain runs: come to your house, come to your house, you have no enemies here; your sisters are beside you, weeping.`},{heading:"How they were sung",body:`The instructions are remarkable. The Berlin papyrus directs that the songs be sung on the festival of Osiris, in the month of Khoiak. Two women, pure in body and virgins according to the text, should have the hair of their bodies removed, their heads adorned with wigs, and tambourines in their hands. The name of Isis is to be written on the arm of one, and the name of Nephthys on the arm of the other. They sing before the god, in the temple, through the festival.

So the voice of Nephthys was given to a real woman, chosen and purified, who sang it. Her grief was performed by people for the god, year after year, as it was performed by the two kites at every funeral.`},{heading:"What the songs do",body:`The Lamentations are not only sad. They are a ritual of calling back. By naming Osiris, praising him and grieving his loss, the singers draw him home: to his body, his temple and his festival. In Egyptian thinking the right words, rightly spoken, were effective. Grief, spoken well, had power.

That is perhaps the most useful thing a modern devotee can take from Nephthys. Her grief is not passive collapse. It is a practice, a set of words and acts done with others, that helps the dead go where they need to go and helps the living go on.`},{heading:"Keening today",body:`Ritual lamentation by women has a long life around the Mediterranean and beyond: Irish keening, Greek moirologia, the professional mourners of many cultures. Egypt is one of the oldest recorded examples. Many of these traditions have faded, and modern grief is often silent and private.

You do not have to wail to honour Nephthys. But you might learn something from her about grieving out loud, with others, with words: saying the names of the dead, speaking what they were, telling them to come home in peace. That is ancient, and it is also very human.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Papyrus Berlin 3008 and the Bremner-Rhind papyrus (British Museum EA 10188) preserve the songs and their performance instructions; Faulkner published the Bremner-Rhind songs in 1936. Professional mourners and the two kites at funerals are shown in tomb paintings from many periods.",unknown:"The surviving papyri are late, and how old the songs are, and how widely they were sung outside major temples, is uncertain. We do not know the melodies. Whether the performers were priestesses, temple musicians or others varied by place."},inPractice:"Read a translation of the Lamentations or the Songs of Isis and Nephthys. Write a short lament of your own for someone you have lost: their name, what they were, and a call for them to go in peace. Read it aloud at her shrine one evening, with the lamp lit.",reflect:["How do you grieve: aloud or in silence, alone or with others? What would you change?","What would you want sung over you?"],further:['R. O. Faulkner, "The Bremner-Rhind Papyrus: I. The Songs of Isis and Nephthys", Journal of Egyptian Archaeology 22 (1936)',"Miriam Lichtheim, Ancient Egyptian Literature, vol. 3: The Late Period (1980), for the Lamentations of Isis and Nephthys","John H. Taylor, Death and the Afterlife in Ancient Egypt (2001)"],library:["traditional/deity/nephthys","traditional/deity/osiris","traditional/deity/isis"],premade:["hedge-ancestor-candle-evening"]},{id:"nephthys-cult",title:"Traces of Her Own Worship",summary:"Where Nephthys was honoured, the lamps of the night, and what it means to serve a goddess with little surviving cult.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"A goddess beside others",body:`For most of Egyptian history Nephthys was honoured beside Isis and Osiris, in their temples and in funerary religion, rather than in great temples of her own. She appears in Osiris chapels, on coffins, in tomb paintings and in the festival texts of Khoiak. In the temples of Isis at Philae and of Hathor at Dendera, she stands with her sister in many scenes.

There are traces of a cult of her own. Land records of the Ramesside period, especially the great Wilbour Papyrus, mention a house of Nephthys at a place in Middle Egypt called Sepermeru, close to a temple of Set, with its own lands and staff. In the Late Period and Ptolemaic times she had shrines in several towns. But nothing like Philae or Dendera survives for her alone.`},{heading:"Lamps in the night",body:`Nephthys is often linked with night and with lamps in modern practice. The ancient evidence for that is indirect but real. Egyptian funerary religion kept lamps for the dead; some Book of the Dead spells are spoken over torches, and the four torches of the burial chamber guard the dead. Herodotus, who visited Egypt in the fifth century BCE, describes a night festival at Sais, the city of Neith, when people all over Egypt burned many lamps around their houses all night, in connection with mourning for a god he will not name, almost certainly Osiris.

Nephthys is not named in Herodotus. But a lamp burned through the night for the dead god, in the season of mourning, is very much her territory, and it is one of the most fitting things a modern devotee can offer her.`},{heading:"The household",body:`Because of her name, many modern devotees honour Nephthys as a goddess of the household: keeping the home, guarding its boundaries, caring for those within it. The ancient sources do not clearly support that as her main role, but they do not forbid it. "Lady of the House" was also a title of an Egyptian married woman, nebet per, the mistress of the household, though the word for house there is different.

If you honour her as lady of your home, do it with that honesty: a modern reading of an old name. Keeping a home in order, making it a safe place for grief, opening it to someone who needs shelter, are all good offerings.`},{heading:"Serving a goddess with gaps",body:`Much about Nephthys is lost. That can make people uneasy, and it can also tempt them to fill the gaps with invention: secret rites, hidden teachings, a "dark goddess" mythology. Some of that is creative and moving. But it helps to keep a line between what Egyptians recorded and what we imagine.

Serving a goddess with gaps is a practice of its own. You keep what is known: the water, the bread, the lamp, the names of the dead, the place beside her sister. You hold the rest open. In a way that suits her: the one who stays beside what is unfinished, and does not need to fill the silence.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Nephthys appears in temple scenes at Philae, Dendera and elsewhere; the Wilbour Papyrus records her house at Sepermeru; Herodotus 2.62 describes the festival of lamps at Sais; funerary lamps and torches appear in Book of the Dead spells and burials.",unknown:"How large or important her own cult places were is uncertain. Herodotus does not name Nephthys, and the link between his lamp festival and her is a modern inference. Her role as a household goddess is a modern reading of her name."},inPractice:"Give her a lamp as your main offering this week: a few minutes of light each evening, put out before you sleep. Make a short list of what is known of her and keep it on her shrine; let the rest be quiet. Do one act of care for your home or for someone who needs shelter.",reflect:["How do you feel about honouring a goddess about whom so much is lost?","What does your home need from you to be a good place for grief, or for rest?"],further:["Herodotus, Histories 2.62, trans. G. C. Macaulay (1890), Project Gutenberg ebook 2707","Sally L. D. Katary, Land Tenure in the Ramesside Period (1989), on the Wilbour Papyrus","Emily Teeter, Religion and Ritual in Ancient Egypt (2011)"],library:["traditional/deity/nephthys","traditional/candle/white","traditional/crystal/moonstone"]}],practices:[{id:"nephthys-evening-water",title:"Evening water and lamp",instructions:"Each evening, wash your hands, uncover her shrine, light her lamp for a few minutes, pour fresh water into her bowl, raise your hands and say one line of praise. Pour yesterday\u2019s water on the earth. Put out the lamp and cover the shrine before you leave it.",times:11,every:"day",prompt:"One line: what did you carry into the evening?"}]},{kind:"practice",title:"Ten Evening Vigils",intro:"For ten evenings, one Egyptian ten-day week, you keep a short vigil at her shrine with a lamp, an offering and a theme. Read the lesson on honouring her today before you begin.",days:10,lessons:[{id:"nephthys-today",title:"Nephthys Today: Practice, Days and Care",summary:"Modern communities, how to offer, her days, and the sensitive points.",minutes:7,sections:[{heading:"Who honours her now",body:`Kemetic religion, the modern revival of Egyptian religion, honours Nephthys, usually beside Isis and Osiris. Reconstructionists often keep her in a shrine for the akhu, the blessed dead, and sing or read laments for Osiris in the season of Khoiak. Kemetic Orthodoxy, a temple founded in 1988 by Tamara Siuda, honours her in its calendar, and some of its members are devoted to her; they follow that temple's customs.

Outside Kemetic religion, many Pagans and witches honour Nephthys as a goddess of grief, death, the night and transitions, and often as a "dark" counterpart of Isis. Hospice workers, death doulas and people in mourning are drawn to her from many paths. Each practice is its own; say honestly which one you keep.`},{heading:"What to offer and how",body:`Water, bread and beer are the offerings of every Egyptian god, and suit her. Cool water is especially fitting for a goddess of the dead, since water was poured for the dead in Egypt. Myrrh and frankincense; a strip of clean white linen, the cloth of mummies and temples; and above all, light. A lamp lit for her and for the dead is a lovely offering.

Use the Egyptian shape: purify, light, open, praise, offer, sit, close, and afterward eat or share the bread, the reversion of offerings. In her spirit, presence is an offering: sitting with someone who is grieving, visiting the sick or the dying, writing to a friend who has lost someone, tending a grave.`},{heading:"Her days",body:`Her birthday is the fifth and last of the epagomenal days, the days upon the year before Wep Ronpet, the new year. Her great season is Khoiak, the month of the Osiris mysteries, when the Lamentations of Isis and Nephthys were sung. The Wag festival of the dead, around the 18th of the first month, suits her too, as do the anniversaries of your own dead.

Modern Kemetic calendars line these up differently, depending on how they fix the new year. Choose one calendar and keep it from year to year, and keep a few days well rather than many badly.`},{heading:"Sensitive points",body:`Nephthys is a goddess for the grieving, and grief can be heavy. Keep rites short when you are raw. If you find that her practice is keeping you in grief rather than helping you carry it, take a break, and talk to someone. If grief becomes too much, or you have thoughts of harming yourself, please reach out: your doctor or a bereavement service, a crisis line (988 in the US, Samaritans on 116 123 in the UK and Ireland), or your local emergency number.

Do not use her to contact the dead or to frighten yourself. Egyptian care of the dead was offering and remembrance, not summoning. And be careful with modern lore presented as ancient. You can love the "dark goddess" Nephthys of modern Paganism; just know that she is a modern figure built on an old name.`}],knowAndDont:{known:"Kemetic Orthodoxy and other Kemetic communities are documented modern movements. The epagomenal birthdays, the Khoiak laments, the Wag festival and water offerings for the dead are well attested.",unknown:"There is no single modern Kemetic calendar, and ancient festival dates drifted against the seasons. Her modern roles as a goddess of the night, the household and the hidden are modern developments from her name and myth."},inPractice:"Fix your offering habit: water and a lamp most evenings, bread weekly, a fuller offering and the names of your dead on her days. Put her birthday, Khoiak and the anniversaries of your dead in your calendar. Choose one act of presence for someone grieving this month.",reflect:["Who in your life is grieving now, and how could you sit with them?","How will you know if this practice is helping your grief, or keeping you stuck in it?"],further:["Richard J. Reidy, Eternal Egypt: Ancient Rituals for the Modern World (2010)","John H. Taylor, Death and the Afterlife in Ancient Egypt (2001)"],library:["traditional/deity/nephthys","traditional/crystal/obsidian","traditional/crystal/amethyst","traditional/candle/black"],premade:["hedge-letting-go-of-the-dead"]}],practices:[{id:"nephthys-ten-vigils",title:"Ten evening vigils",instructions:`Each evening: wash your hands and face, uncover the shrine, light her lamp and the incense, raise your hands and greet her. Give water and bread. Then sit with the evening's theme for five to ten minutes by the lamp, and journal. Thank her, cover the shrine and put out the lamp. Eat or share the bread tomorrow.

Evening 1: the Lady of the House; what house do you keep? Evening 2: the sister who stayed. Evening 3: the choice against her husband; a loyalty you chose. Evening 4: the two kites; let yourself grieve aloud, even briefly. Evening 5: the head of the bier; speak the names of your dead. Evening 6: the nurse of Horus; someone young you protect or could. Evening 7: the Lamentations; read or write a short lament. Evening 8: the lamps of Sais; let the lamp burn a little longer for those who died alone. Evening 9: the gaps; sit in silence with what is unknown. Evening 10: what you would ask of her, and what you would offer back.

If you miss an evening, carry on where you left off. A battery lamp is a fine alternative to a flame.`,times:10,every:"day",prompt:"What did tonight\u2019s theme bring up, and whose name did you speak?"}]},{kind:"discernment",title:"Keeping Vigil",intro:"Take a few quiet days with only the evening water and lamp. Read your journal from the beginning. Dedication is one good outcome; so is pausing, stopping, or finding that another god, perhaps Isis, Osiris or Anubis, is the one calling you.",days:3,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"nephthys-reread",title:"Read by lamplight",instructions:"One evening, light her lamp and read your journal from the first entry, slowly. Mark the moments that felt alive and the ones that felt heavy. Then answer the questions below honestly.",times:1,prompt:"What has this month with Nephthys given you, and what has it asked of you?"}],questions:["Are you drawn to Nephthys herself, or mainly to what she offers in a time of grief? Both are honest, but they may lead to different choices.","Is your interest coming from fresh grief? If so, would it be wiser to keep her close without a vow for now, and decide when the grief has settled?","Can you keep a small, steady practice (water, a lamp, the names of your dead, her days) through an ordinary year?","Would a Kemetic community, or practical work with the grieving and the dying, serve what you are looking for better than a self-dedication?","If you decide to wait or stop, can you thank her, give a last offering and take down the shrine with care? That is a good ending too."]},{kind:"dedication",title:"The Dedication",intro:"If you choose to go on, this is your self-dedication to Nephthys: a promise you write and speak yourself. It is not an initiation into Kemetic Orthodoxy or any other group, and it does not replace one. An evening after dark is best; her birthday on the last epagomenal day, a night in Khoiak, or the anniversary of someone you have lost are all fitting.",days:1,lessons:[],practices:[],rite:{title:"Self-Dedication to Nephthys",summary:"A full evening rite in the Egyptian shape: purification, the lamp, opening, adoration, offering, the names of the dead, the vow, a vigil, the reversion and closing.",minutes:45,ingredients:[{name:"Nephthys",ref:"traditional/deity/nephthys"},{name:"An offering bowl of fresh water",ref:"traditional/vessel/offering_bowl"},{name:"Natron, washing soda or salt in a cup of water, for washing"},{name:"Bread, and beer or another drink",ref:"traditional/vessel/dish"},{name:"Myrrh or frankincense and an incense burner",ref:"traditional/herb/myrrh"},{name:"An oil lamp, or a white or silver candle in a glass holder",ref:"traditional/candle/white"},{name:"A strip of clean white linen"},{name:"The names of your dead, written out",optional:!0},{name:"A sistrum, rattle or bell",optional:!0},{name:"Your vow, written by hand"}],preparation:`During the day, write your vow: what you will do (for example, evening water and a lamp, the names of your dead, her days kept, one act of presence for the grieving each month), for how long (a year and a day is common), and what you hope for. Keep it short and keepable.

Before the rite, bathe, rinse your mouth with natron or salt water, and dress in clean, preferably white or linen clothes. Clean the shrine and set everything out, with her image covered. Fold the linen neatly.`,steps:[{title:"Purify and light",instructions:"Light her lamp and the incense. Wash your hands in the natron water, touch it to your lips, and pass your hands through the smoke.",light:!0,spoken:"I am pure, I am pure. I have washed, I have passed through the smoke. I come to the Lady of the House with clean hands and a lamp lit."},{title:"Open the shrine",instructions:"Uncover her image slowly. Shake the sistrum or ring the bell softly if you have one.",spoken:"The doors are opened. Your face is uncovered, Nebet-Het. Awake in peace."},{title:"Adore",instructions:"Raise both hands, palms toward her, and speak the praise slowly.",spoken:"Homage to you, Nebet-Het, Lady of the House, daughter of Geb and Nut, sister of Aset and Wesir. You who chose what was right, who wept as a kite, who sat at the head of the bier and spread your wings over the dead, who nursed the child Heru: I praise you."},{title:"Offer",instructions:"Lift the water, the bread and the drink toward her and set them down. Lay the linen before her image.",spoken:"An offering for Nebet-Het: water and bread, drink and sweet smoke, clean linen and a lamp in the night, every good and pure thing. May it please you."},{title:"The names",instructions:"If you wish, read the names of your dead aloud, slowly, pausing after each.",minutes:5,spoken:"Watch over them, Nebet-Het, at the head of the bier. Let their names live."},{title:"Speak your vow",instructions:"Read your written vow aloud, clearly. Sign and date it.",spoken:"This is my word, given freely and in truth. I will keep it as well as I am able. Hear me, Nebet-Het."},{title:"Keep vigil",instructions:"Sit by the lamp in silence. Do not hunt for signs; simply stay, as she stays.",minutes:10},{title:"Reversion and closing",instructions:"Thank her. Eat a piece of the bread. Lay your vow on the linen at the foot of her image. Cover the shrine and put out the lamp. Step back before you turn away.",snuff:!0,spoken:"What you have received, I share. Rest in peace, Nebet-Het. I am yours, and I will return."}],closing:"Pour the water on the earth the next morning. Keep the linen and your vow on her shrine. Record the rite in your journal. On the anniversaries of your dead, light her lamp and reread your vow.",safety:"Keep the lamp, candle and incense on heatproof surfaces in a ventilated room, away from curtains, children, pets and pet birds, and never leave them burning unattended or overnight. Keep the linen well away from the flame. Do not drink alcohol if you should not. If the rite brings up grief you cannot carry, stop gently and reach out to someone."}},{kind:"ongoing",title:"The Lamp in the House",intro:"After the dedication comes the quiet, steady part: water and a lamp in the evening, the dead remembered, her days kept, presence offered to the grieving, and a yearly renewal of your vow.",days:365,lessons:[],practices:[{id:"nephthys-daily",title:"Evening water and lamp",instructions:"Most evenings, give fresh water, light her lamp for a few minutes, raise your hands and say a line of praise. Put out the lamp before you leave it. On hard days, the water and a single word are enough.",times:365,every:"day"},{id:"nephthys-month",title:"Monthly remembrance",instructions:"Once a month, at the dark of the moon if you like, give bread, incense and the offering formula for your dead, speak their names, and do one act of presence for someone grieving.",times:12,every:"month",prompt:"Whose name did you speak this month, and whom did you sit with?"},{id:"nephthys-holy-days",title:"Her holy days",instructions:`Keep her birthday on the fifth epagomenal day with a fuller offering, linen and a lamp. In Khoiak, read or sing part of the Lamentations of Isis and Nephthys for Osiris, and keep a lamp burning through an evening for the dead. Keep the Wag festival and the anniversaries of your own dead at her shrine.

Use the calendar you have chosen, and keep it from year to year.`,times:3,every:"holy-day",prompt:"How did you keep the day, and whom did you remember?"},{id:"nephthys-renewal",title:"Yearly rededication",instructions:"On the anniversary of your dedication, read the year\u2019s journal, write a new vow, and repeat the dedication rite. Change the promise honestly. If you are ending the dedication, thank her, give a last offering and take down the shrine with care.",times:1,prompt:"What has a year at her side taught you about staying?"}]}],sources:["Pyramid Texts on Isis and Nephthys as the two kites, trans. R. O. Faulkner, The Ancient Egyptian Pyramid Texts (1969), and James P. Allen, The Ancient Egyptian Pyramid Texts (2nd edn, 2015)","The Lamentations of Isis and Nephthys (Papyrus Berlin 3008), in Miriam Lichtheim, Ancient Egyptian Literature, vol. 3 (1980)","The Songs of Isis and Nephthys (Bremner-Rhind papyrus, British Museum EA 10188), trans. R. O. Faulkner, Journal of Egyptian Archaeology 22 (1936)","Plutarch, On Isis and Osiris \xA7\xA712, 14 and 38, trans. F. C. Babbitt (Loeb, 1936; public domain), LacusCurtius, on her birth, Anubis and the borders of the land","Herodotus, Histories 2.62 (the festival of lamps at Sais), trans. G. C. Macaulay (1890), Project Gutenberg ebook 2707","The Wilbour Papyrus (Brooklyn Museum 34.5596), on the house of Nephthys at Sepermeru; Sally L. D. Katary, Land Tenure in the Ramesside Period (1989)","Richard H. Wilkinson, The Complete Gods and Goddesses of Ancient Egypt (2003); Geraldine Pinch, Egyptian Mythology (2002); John H. Taylor, Death and the Afterlife in Ancient Egypt (2001)"]};return u(f);})();
